# Vicem Context Baton & AST Diet Rule

Use this rule when handing off active coding sessions across AI agents (Cursor, Claude Code, OpenAI Codex CLI, Gemini CLI, Windsurf, or Web AI), rescuing bloated sessions from context rot, or working with `.vicem/BATON.md` and `.vicem/ledger.json`.

## Core Workflow

1. **Reading a Mounted Handoff (`.vicem/BATON.md`)**
   - Always read `.vicem/BATON.md` first and resume directly from **Section 2 (`Immediate NEXT #1`)**.
   - Never repeat completed work listed under **Section 3 (`Done`)**.

2. **Lossless AST Skeletons & On-Demand Rehydration (`vicem_rehydrate`)**
   - Unchanged functions (`>= 12` lines) inside `.vicem/BATON.md` are represented as deterministic AST skeletons (`{ /* ... */ }` or `pass`) to save 80–99% of context tokens while keeping all imports, types, and signatures intact.
   - Before editing a skeletonized symbol, call the MCP tool `vicem_rehydrate(file_path, symbol_name)` (or read the source file directly) to inspect the exact implementation.
   - **Never write skeleton placeholders (`{ /* ... */ }`, `pass`, or `‹REDACTED›`) into source files on disk.**

3. **Handing Off or Compacting the Session**
   - Run `npx -y vicem-cli codex` (or `claude`, `cursor`, `gemini`, `windsurf`) to pack `.vicem/BATON.md` and hand off to the target agent.
   - Run `npx -y vicem-cli gui` to review the handoff interactively before delivery.
   - Run `npx -y vicem-cli self` to compact a bloated session in place without losing architectural context.
